DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers the conceptual distinctions and sub-debates that
structure the broader dispute between appropriationism and
contextualism in the historiography of philosophy. Several overlapping
fault lines run through the field. First, the distinction between
"history of philosophy" (focused on philosophical arguments as
arguments) and "history of ideas" (focused on units of thought across
historical contexts, as theorized by Arthur O. Lovejoy) marks a
disciplinary boundary with methodological consequences: historians of
ideas treat texts as vehicles for tracing idea-units, while
philosophers tend to evaluate whether arguments are valid. Second, the
Cambridge School's insistence on linguistic context and authorial
intention (Skinner, Pocock, Dunn) introduced a third orientation --
intellectual history -- that differs from both by emphasizing illocutionary
acts and the political languages of historical speech. Third, analytic
and continental traditions have developed quite different
self-understandings of their relationship to their own histories:
analytic philosophers have historically been suspicious of historical
methods (though a "historical turn" in analytic philosophy has partly
changed this), while continental philosophers tend to see historical
engagement with past texts as philosophically constitutive, not
merely contextual. Fourth, hermeneutics (Gadamer, Ricoeur) offers a
philosophical theory of how understanding across temporal distance is
possible, with direct implications for what it means to interpret
historical texts charitably or critically. These sub-debates
collectively constrain and inform the appropriationism/contextualism
debate by raising the question: what kind of enterprise is the history
of philosophy, and for whom?

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
The key anchor volume (Laerke, Smith, Schliesser, OUP) presupposes
these distinctions and positions itself at their intersection; the
papers in this domain explain the intellectual terrain in which that
volume intervenes. Understanding the Cambridge School, the
analytic-continental divide in historiography, and hermeneutics is
essential for understanding why the appropriationism/contextualism
debate takes the shape it does and what is at stake in different
methodological choices.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Lovejoy's own methodological writings from the 1930s-1950s are not
directly represented (Great Chain of Being 1936 is a book without a
findable DOI for the original monograph; only secondary literature is
included). The debate about feminist historiography of philosophy
(Alanen, Witt) and its methodological distinctives is underrepresented.
Two entries marked INCOMPLETE (no abstract obtainable via API): skinner1969meaning (Skinner 1969, History and Theory) and frede1988history (Frede 1988, Journal of Philosophy); both are included in the file but excluded from synthesis.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
These papers should be read as a map of the meta-methodological
landscape within which the appropriationism/contextualism debate
operates; the synthesis writer should use them to situate that debate
within the broader field of intellectual history and hermeneutics
rather than treating it as an isolated intraphilosophical dispute.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Cambridge School contextualism: 3 papers -- Skinner's speech-act approach
  to intellectual history as the dominant alternative to philosophical presentism
- Analytic-continental divide: 5 papers -- competing accounts of why analytic
  and continental philosophy parted ways and what this means for historiography
- Hermeneutics: 3 papers -- Gadamer- and Ricoeur-influenced accounts of
  interpretation across temporal distance
- History of ideas vs history of philosophy: 4 papers -- demarcating Lovejoy's
  method from both analytic historiography and Cambridge contextualism
- Analytic historiography's self-understanding: 3 papers -- the recent
  historical turn in analytic philosophy and its methodological implications
====================================================================
}

@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: The Aims and Function of the History of Philosophy
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-01
PAPERS_FOUND: 18 total (High: 9, Medium: 7, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, CrossRef (verification), WebSearch
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Debate about the aims and function of history of philosophy is itself a meta-philosophical
enterprise, asking why philosophers should engage the past at all and what that engagement
is supposed to accomplish. The central positions form a spectrum. At one end, appropriationists
(sometimes called "Whig historians") hold that the history of philosophy is philosophically
valuable insofar as it supplies arguments and positions that can be taken up, reconstructed,
and deployed in present-day philosophical debate---the past serves the present. At the other end,
historicists and contextualists (broadly Skinnerian and hermeneutic) insist that past texts
must be understood on their own terms, in their intellectual and social context, and that
presentist readings distort rather than illuminate. A third cluster of positions, neither purely
appropriationist nor purely contextual, assigns the history of philosophy more distinctive
functions: as defamiliarization (making present assumptions strange, Perler), as genealogical
critique of the present (Williams, Geuss), as therapeutic dissolution of pseudo-problems
(Fischer, Wittgenstein-influenced currents), or as constitutive of philosophical self-understanding
(Glock, Cohen). Rorty's 1984 typology of four genres---rational reconstruction, historical
reconstruction, doxography, and Geistesgeschichte---remains a touchstone because it explicitly
maps the tension between philosophical and historical aims onto different modes of
historiographical practice.

The collection Philosophy in History (Rorty, Schneewind, Skinner 1984) launched the
contemporary Anglophone debate by bringing together philosophers who took seriously
that history of philosophy has its own methodological questions. The 2013 collection
edited by Laerke, Smith, and Schliesser revisited these issues specifically for early modern
philosophy, while the British Academy volume Philosophy and the Historical Perspective
(van Ackeren 2018) brought together analytical and continental voices. Glock's 2008 article
in Mind provides the most systematic survey of reasons for and against treating history of
philosophy as philosophically relevant. Watson, Nichols, and Cohen represent the self-
reflective tradition of asking explicitly whether history of philosophy has intrinsic value
or only instrumental value reducible to doing philosophy.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain directly concerns the meta-philosophical foundations of the broader
historiography debate: before asking how to do history of philosophy (the contextualism vs.
appropriationism debate), one must ask why to do it at all and what one hopes to achieve.
Positions on aims constrain admissible methods---if the goal is genealogical critique,
historical accuracy is constitutive; if the goal is rational reconstruction for present debate,
textual fidelity may be subordinated. The Laerke/Smith/Schliesser volume is the primary
anchor source for this project.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The debate has been substantially dominated by Anglophone (especially analytic) voices;
the German tradition of Geistesgeschichte and the French histoire de la philosophie as a
genre distinct from philosophy receive less attention. Feminist historiography of philosophy
as a corrective practice (recovering marginalized voices) is largely absent from the texts
surveyed.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The four-genre typology from Rorty 1984 can serve as a structural scaffold for
distinguishing different functional conceptions; synthesis should show how positions on
function map onto positions on method (contextualist vs. appropriationist).

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Appropriationist/rational reconstruction: [4 papers] - Past texts as resources for present argument
- Historicist/contextualist: [3 papers] - Past on its own terms, historical accuracy paramount
- Defamiliarization/genealogical critique: [3 papers] - History makes the present strange
- Constitutive/intrinsic value: [4 papers] - History essential to philosophy's self-understanding
- Therapeutic dissolution: [2 papers] - History as resolving pseudo-problems
====================================================================
}

@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Methodological Practice --- Anachronism, Charity, Canon, Sources, Translation
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-01
PAPERS_FOUND: 18 total (High: 8, Medium: 7, Low: 3)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef verification
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers the concrete interpretive practices and craft issues that define the historiography of philosophy as a methodological discipline. Four interlocking problem areas emerge: (1) the principle of charity and its variants (Davidson, Grandy), which raises the question of whether maximizing rational coherence in a historical text is an epistemic prerequisite or a distortion; (2) the problem of anachronism---whether applying contemporary concepts to historical texts is avoidable, and whether it might sometimes be productive rather than merely erroneous; (3) canon formation and its critiques, including feminist and decolonial challenges to the exclusion of women and non-Western thinkers; and (4) translation and linguistic commensurability as methodological obstacles in cross-cultural and cross-temporal philosophy.

The charity-anachronism cluster is tightly connected to the appropriationism vs. contextualism debate (Domain 1). Davidson's principle of charity, originally developed for radical interpretation in philosophy of language, has been applied (and challenged) in the history of philosophy. Richard Grandy's principle of humanity offers a related but importantly different formulation. Tom Stern's analysis of charity in Nietzsche interpretation, and the SEP article on Davidson, represent the most direct engagements with this question in a historiographic context. Anachronism as a methodological problem has been addressed from multiple directions: Jardine's historical-scientific perspective, Pickave's defence of productive anachronism, Harari's distinction between historical and philosophical contextualisation, and the OpenAlex-found article by Kaukua and Lahteenmaki on non-textual interpretive standards.

Canon critique is now a major field. Christia Mercer's 2019 article traces the "contextualist revolution" in early modern studies, while her 2016 piece on Descartes and Teresa of Avila makes the case for recovering women in the history of philosophy. Charlotte Witt and Eileen O'Neill are key figures in feminist historiography. Sarah Tyson's analysis of reclamation strategies and Kristie Dotson's challenge to professional exclusions represent more recent interventions. Bryan Van Norden's manifesto argues for multicultural inclusion of non-Western philosophy. Jonardon Ganeri's 2025 piece proposes a cosmopolitan post-Eurocentric framework for the discipline as a whole.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain directly supplies the methodological craft vocabulary for the broader appropriationism vs. contextualism debate: how charity is applied to historical texts, when anachronism is a vice or a productive tool, and how canon selection shapes what counts as the "history of philosophy" to be interpreted. These issues are presupposed by or embedded within the methodological positions debated in the anchor volume.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Work on translation as a specific methodological problem in cross-cultural history of philosophy (as opposed to incommensurability in philosophy of science) remains relatively underdeveloped in the literature found; the Ganeri 2025 piece is the closest to this concern but focuses on cosmopolitanism rather than translation practice. The Sorell and Rogers (2005) volume (Analytic Philosophy and History of Philosophy, OUP) was not found via CrossRef verification and could not be confirmed, so it is omitted. Eileen O'Neill's chapter "Disappearing Ink" (1997, in Philosophy in a Feminist Voice) could not be verified by CrossRef and is omitted per citation integrity rules; her work is represented through the verified Mercer piece that builds on it.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
This domain provides the "lower-level" methodological vocabulary (charity, anachronism, canon) that feeds into the "higher-level" debate between appropriationism and contextualism. Synthesis should connect these concrete practices to the theoretical positions of Domain 1 and the figures in Domain 2, treating them as case studies or illustrations of those positions.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Charity as interpretive principle: 3 papers (Davidson, Grandy, Stern)
- Anachronism debate: 4 papers (Jardine, Pickave/PhilPapers, Harari, Kaukua/Lahteenmaki)
- Feminist canon critique: 4 papers (Witt, Tyson, Dotson, Mercer 2016)
- Non-Western/decolonial canon critique: 2 papers (Van Norden, Ganeri)
- Contextualism vs. appropriationism methodology: 3 papers (Skinner, Mercer 2019, Ferreira da Silva)
- Analytic vs. historical approaches: 2 papers (Glock, Smith 2024)
====================================================================
}

@book{rortySchneewindSkinner1984philosophyHistory,
  editor = {Rorty, Richard and Schneewind, Jerome B. and Skinner, Quentin},
  title = {Philosophy in History},
  year = {1984},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511625534},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This edited volume collects foundational essays on how
  philosophers should engage with the philosophical past, including
  Rorty's programmatic "The Historiography of Philosophy: Four Genres"
  (pp. 49-76), which distinguishes rational reconstruction, historical
  reconstruction, Geistesgeschichte, and doxography. Rorty argues that
  rational reconstruction -- treating past thinkers as having answers
  to our questions -- is legitimate as one genre alongside, not instead
  of, historical reconstruction.

  RELEVANCE: This volume is the locus classicus for the framing of the
  appropriationism/contextualism debate in Anglophone philosophy;
  virtually all subsequent methodological literature responds to it.
  Rorty's four-genre typology has structured the debate for four
  decades.

  POSITION: The volume establishes the pluralist framing that both
  sides of the debate have subsequently contested or defended.
  },
  keywords = {historiography-of-philosophy, appropriationism, contextualism, rational-reconstruction, foundational, High},
  abstract = {The sixteen essays in this volume confront the current debate about the relationship between philosophy and its history. On the one hand intellectual historians commonly accuse philosophers of writing bad - anachronistic - history of philosophy, and on the other, philosophers have accused intellectual historians of writing bad - antiquarian - history of philosophy. The essays here address this controversy and ask what purpose the history of philosophy should serve. Part I contains more purely theoretical and methodological discussion, of such questions as whether there are 'timeless' philosophical problems, whether the issues of one epoch are commensurable with those of another, and what style is appropriate to the historiography of the subject. The essays in Part II consider a number of case-histories. They present important revisionist scholarship and original contributions on topics drawn from ancient, early modern and more recent philosophy. All the essays have been specially commissioned, and the contributors include many of the leading figures in the field. The volume as a whole will be of vital interest to everyone concerned with the study of philosophy and of its history.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{rorty1984historiography,
  author = {Rorty, Richard},
  title = {The historiography of philosophy: four genres},
  booktitle = {Philosophy in History},
  year = {1984},
  pages = {49--76},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511625534.006},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rorty distinguishes four genres of writing about
  philosophical history: rational reconstruction (making past
  philosophers interlocutors in current debates), historical
  reconstruction (situating texts in their context), Geistesgeschichte
  (locating philosophy in broader intellectual culture), and
  doxography (textbook summaries). He defends rational reconstruction
  as philosophically fruitful while acknowledging it distorts the
  historical record; the two genres serve different but compatible
  purposes. The essay introduces the vocabulary of "appropriation"
  that has since defined one pole of the debate.

  RELEVANCE: This is the canonical statement of the appropriationist
  position, and the primary target of contextualist responses by
  Garber, Mercer, and others. Any treatment of the debate must engage
  directly with this text.

  POSITION: Appropriationism/rational reconstruction.
  },
  keywords = {historiography-of-philosophy, appropriationism, rational-reconstruction, Rorty, High},
  abstract = {Analytic philosophers who have attempted ‘rational reconstructions’ of the arguments of great dead philosophers have done so in the hope of treating these philosophers as contemporaries, as colleagues with whom they can exchange views. They have argued that unless one does this one might as well turn over the history of philosophy to historians – whom they picture as mere doxographers, rather than seekers after philosophical truth. Such reconstructions, however, have led to charges of anachronism. Analytic historians of philosophy are frequently accused of beating texts into the shape of propositions currently being debated in the philosophical journals. It is urged that we should not force Aristotle or Kant to take sides in current debates within philosophy of language or metaethics. There seems to be a dilemma: either we anachronistically impose enough of our problems and vocabulary on the dead to make them conversational partners, or we confine our interpretive activity to making their falsehoods look less silly by placing them in the context of the benighted times in which they were written.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{skinner1969meaningUnderstanding,
  author = {Skinner, Quentin},
  title = {Meaning and Understanding in the History of Ideas},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {1969},
  volume = {8},
  number = {1},
  pages = {3},
  doi = {10.2307/2504188},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Skinner attacks three "mythologies" that distort the
  history of ideas: the mythology of doctrines (reading a thinker as
  having a doctrine on questions they never addressed), the mythology
  of coherence (assuming that any thinker must hold a consistent
  system), and the mythology of prolepsis (treating earlier thinkers
  as anticipating later ones). Against these, Skinner argues that
  understanding a text requires recovering the author's intention in
  the context of the specific language games available to them; meaning
  is tied to illocutionary acts performed within a linguistic context.

  RELEVANCE: This article is the founding document of the Cambridge
  School of contextualism and the primary source for arguments against
  appropriationist history of philosophy. Skinner's three mythologies
  are the main charges appropriationists must answer.

  POSITION: Contextualism -- the locus classicus of the view.
  },
  keywords = {historiography-of-philosophy, contextualism, Skinner, methodology, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

