  },
  keywords = {historiography-of-philosophy, methodology, edited-volume, anchor-source, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{skinner2002visions,
  author = {Skinner, Quentin},
  title = {Visions of Politics, Volume 1: Regarding Method},
  year = {2002},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511790812},
  abstract = {The first of three volumes of essays by Quentin Skinner, one of the world's leading intellectual historians. This collection includes some of his most important philosophical and methodological statements written over the past four decades, each carefully revised for publication in this form. In a series of seminal essays Professor Skinner sets forth the intellectual principles that inform his work, engaging with critics who have challenged him from both analytic and hermeneutic perspectives.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Skinner defends a speech-act-theoretic methodology for intellectual history according to which the interpreter's primary task is to recover the illocutionary point of a historical utterance: what the author was doing in saying what they said, within the linguistic conventions available to them. This rules out anachronistic attribution of questions or doctrines not available to the historical agent, and equally rules out reading texts as contributions to perennial problems. The volume gathers essays from "Meaning and Understanding" (1969) through later refinements.

  RELEVANCE: This is the canonical methodological statement of Cambridge School contextualism as it applies to the history of political thought; its arguments directly target the kind of appropriationism that takes historical texts as contributions to timeless debates. The distinction between what Skinner calls "historical" and "philosophical" questions defines one horn of the appropriationism/contextualism divide.

  POSITION: Cambridge School contextualism; anti-appropriationist.
  },
  keywords = {Cambridge-School, contextualism, intellectual-history, methodology, High}
}

@article{mandelbaum1965history,
  author = {Mandelbaum, Maurice},
  title = {The History of Ideas, Intellectual History, and the History of Philosophy},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {1965},
  volume = {5},
  pages = {33--66},
  doi = {10.2307/2504118},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mandelbaum distinguishes three disciplinary enterprises that are often conflated: the history of ideas (concerned with the fate of particular ideas across epochs and domains, following Lovejoy), intellectual history (concerned with the thought of an era in its social and cultural context), and history of philosophy (concerned with philosophical arguments and their validity). Each enterprise involves different selection criteria, methods, and standards of success. The history of philosophy is not simply a subset of the history of ideas.

  RELEVANCE: This article provides the clearest classical taxonomy of the sub-fields and explains what is at stake in the choice among them. The appropriationism/contextualism debate presupposes, but rarely makes explicit, exactly these distinctions. Mandelbaum's analysis clarifies why historians and philosophers so often talk past each other about what the history of philosophy is for.

  POSITION: Analytical demarcation; neutral with respect to appropriationism vs. contextualism but essential for situating the debate.
  },
  keywords = {history-of-ideas, intellectual-history, history-of-philosophy, demarcation, High},
  abstract = {Those who are at present interested in methodological problems of historian of philosophy cannot fail to take note of existence of what has come to be called the history of ideas. The latter term has been used in a variety of senses, sometimes being equated with intellectual history generally.1 However, it is not in that wider sense that I wish to consider it. Instead, I shall in first instance confine my attention to problems which arise out of two important parallel movements, each of which may be regarded as an attempt to promote study of the history of ideas in a narrower and more technical sense: movement in United States of which A. 0. Lovejoy was originator and dominant spokesman, and movement inspired in Germany by Dilthey's work, and now represented by Erich Rothacker's Archiv fur Begriffsgeschichte. Because Lovejoy and his close collaborators have written at greater length concerning their methodological presuppositions than have those connected with Archiv, and also because these presuppositions seem to raise a greater variety of issues in a somewhat more acute form, it is with former group, rather than latter, that I shall here be concerned.2},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{frede1988history,
  author = {Frede, Michael},
  title = {The History of Philosophy as a Discipline},
  journal = {Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {1988},
  volume = {85},
  number = {11},
  pages = {666--672},
  doi = {10.5840/jphil1988851114},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Frede argues that the history of philosophy has its own disciplinary identity, neither reducible to general historical scholarship nor to systematic philosophy. He contends that doing justice to past philosophers requires both philosophical competence (to understand what is at stake in their arguments) and historical sensitivity (to appreciate what problems they faced and what resources were available to them). The history of philosophy thus occupies a sui generis position between history and philosophy.

  RELEVANCE: Frede's short essay articulates the position that the history of philosophy is distinct from both the history of ideas (contra Lovejoy) and from purely ahistorical philosophical appropriation. It is directly relevant to the question of whether there is a special disciplinary standpoint -- the "historian-philosopher" -- that neither pure historians nor pure philosophers occupy.

  POSITION: Hybrid disciplinary identity; neither pure contextualism nor pure appropriationism.
  },
  keywords = {history-of-philosophy, discipline, methodology, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{catana2010lovejoy,
  author = {Catana, Leo},
  title = {Lovejoy's Readings of Bruno: Or How Nineteenth-century History of Philosophy was ``Transformed'' into the History of Ideas},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Ideas},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {71},
  number = {1},
  pages = {91--112},
  doi = {10.1353/jhi.0.0068},
  abstract = {Arthur O. Lovejoy made rather grand methodological statements about the nature of history of ideas in his Great Chain of Being (1936). These statements were, it is argued, rhetorical declarations, intended to produce the conviction in the minds of his readers that history of ideas was distinct from history of philosophy and thus deserved institutional independence; they were not adequate descriptions of his practice as a historian.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Catana argues that Lovejoy's methodological pronouncements in The Great Chain of Being were strategic rather than accurate descriptions of his actual practice. By tracing Lovejoy's engagement with Bruno, Catana shows that the "unit-idea" method was in practice much closer to nineteenth-century history of philosophy than Lovejoy claimed; the distinction between "history of ideas" and "history of philosophy" was partly motivated by Lovejoy's desire for institutional independence rather than genuine disciplinary difference.

  RELEVANCE: This article directly addresses the history of ideas / history of philosophy distinction from a historical and critical angle, showing that Lovejoy's own foundational distinction is less clean than presented. This complicates the neat taxonomy offered by Mandelbaum and creates space for the hybrid approaches defended by contemporary historians of philosophy.

  POSITION: Critical revision of Lovejoy; questioning the sharpness of the history-of-ideas/history-of-philosophy divide.
  },
  keywords = {Lovejoy, history-of-ideas, history-of-philosophy, Bruno, methodology, Medium}
}

@article{knight2012unit,
  author = {Knight, Carl},
  title = {Unit-Ideas Unleashed: A Reinterpretation and Reassessment of Lovejovian Methodology in the History of Ideas},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {6},
  number = {2},
  pages = {195--217},
  doi = {10.1163/187226312x650746},
  abstract = {This article argues for an unconventional interpretation of Arthur O. Lovejoy's distinctive approach to method in the history of ideas. It is maintained that the value of the central concept of the `unit-idea' has been misunderstood by friends and foes alike. The commonality of unit-ideas at different times and places is often defined in terms of familial resemblance. But such an approach fails to explain the objective unity of the idea.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Knight defends a reinterpretation of Lovejoy's unit-idea method, arguing that critics have misunderstood the basis on which unit-ideas are identified across historical contexts. He proposes that unit-ideas are unified not by familial resemblance (which would be too loose) nor by strict identity (which would be too rigid) but by a more principled criterion. The article thus rehabilitates a modified Lovejovian approach as a viable alternative to both Cambridge contextualism and analytic appropriationism.

  RELEVANCE: Knight's defense of a refined unit-idea methodology provides a contemporary restatement of the "history of ideas" approach as a middle position between narrow contextualism (which resists cross-contextual comparison) and appropriationism (which ignores historical context). This directly populates the methodological space between the poles of the appropriationism/contextualism debate.

  POSITION: Neo-Lovejovian; revisionist defense of the history of ideas.
  },
  keywords = {Lovejoy, unit-ideas, methodology, history-of-ideas, Medium}
}

@article{beaney2016historiography,
  author = {Beaney, Michael},
  title = {Historiography, Philosophy of History and the Historical Turn in Analytic Philosophy},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {10},
  number = {2},
  pages = {211--234},
  doi = {10.1163/18722636-12341321},
  abstract = {This article has three main interconnected aims. First, I illustrate the historiographical conceptions of three early analytic philosophers: Frege, Russell and Wittgenstein. Second, I consider some of the historiographical debates that have been generated by the recent historical turn in analytic philosophy, looking at the work of Scott Soames and Hans-Johann Glock, in particular. Third, I discuss Arthur Danto's Analytic Philosophy of History, published 50 years ago, and argue for a reinvigorated analytic philosophy of history.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Beaney traces how the early analytic philosophers (Frege, Russell, Wittgenstein) each held distinctive historiographical self-understandings -- typically dismissive of their own historical predecessors -- and shows how this shaped analytic philosophy's long-standing suspicion of historical inquiry. He then examines the "historical turn" in analytic philosophy exemplified by debates between Soames and Glock, arguing for a revitalized analytic philosophy of history that combines rigor with historical sensitivity.

  RELEVANCE: This article maps the internal debate within analytic philosophy about its relationship to its own history, directly illuminating what is at stake when analytic philosophers either appropriate or historicize earlier texts. The historical turn Beaney documents is what makes contemporary analytic appropriationism more historically informed than its predecessors.

  POSITION: Historical turn in analytic philosophy; argues for methodological middle ground.
  },
  keywords = {analytic-philosophy, historiography, historical-turn, Frege, Russell, Wittgenstein, High}
}

@article{harrelson2014inferentialist,
  author = {Harrelson, Kevin J.},
  title = {Inferentialist Philosophy of Language and the Historiography of Philosophy},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {22},
  number = {3},
  pages = {582--603},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2014.918022},
  abstract = {This article considers the implications of inferentialist philosophy of language for debates in the historiography of philosophy. My intention is to mediate and refine the polemics between contextualist historians and `analytic' or presentist historians. I claim that much of Robert Brandom's nuanced defence of presentism can be accepted and even adopted by contextualists, so that inferentialism turns out to support neither a straightforwardly presentist nor a straightforwardly contextualist approach.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Harrelson uses Brandom's inferentialist semantics to mediate the debate between contextualists and presentists in the historiography of philosophy, arguing that the inferentialist framework captures what is right in both: texts mean what they normatively commit their authors to, which is partly a matter of context and partly a matter of the inferential role of their claims. Neither pure context nor pure philosophical reconstruction is sufficient.

  RELEVANCE: This article offers a philosophy-of-language grounding for a synthetic position between appropriationism and contextualism, drawing on the very debates within analytic philosophy of language (Brandom vs. Quine/Davidson) that shape contemporary appropriationist arguments. It shows how disputes about linguistic meaning in philosophy of language map onto disputes about historical interpretation.

  POSITION: Inferentialist synthesis; mediating contextualism and presentism.
  },
  keywords = {inferentialism, Brandom, historiography-of-philosophy, contextualism, presentism, Medium}
}

@article{simons2001fault,
  author = {Simons, Peter},
  title = {Whose Fault? The Origins and Evitability of the Analytic--Continental Rift},
  journal = {International Journal of Philosophical Studies},
  year = {2001},
  volume = {9},
  number = {3},
  pages = {295--311},
  doi = {10.1080/09672550110058858},
  abstract = {This is a broad survey of the chronology of the rift between continental and analytic philosophy, starting in 1899. Whereas at that time there was no discernible divide, as the twentieth century progresses we can see a gradual parting of the ways in which philosophy was done, culminating in a period of maximum separation in 1945-68, followed by some convergence.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Simons traces the historical emergence of the analytic-continental rift from 1899 onward, arguing that the divide was not inevitable but resulted from a series of historical contingencies -- institutional, political, and intellectual. He identifies the period 1945-1968 as the period of maximum separation, followed by partial convergence. He assesses responsibility for the rift on both sides.

  RELEVANCE: Understanding the analytic-continental divide as historically contingent rather than philosophically principled is important for assessing whether the different historiographical self-understandings of the two traditions reflect genuine methodological differences or merely disciplinary sociology. This paper contextualizes the "analytic vs. continental" axis of the appropriationism/contextualism debate.

  POSITION: Historical genealogy of the divide; critical of both sides.
  },
  keywords = {analytic-continental, divide, history-of-philosophy, Medium}
}

@article{vrahimis2020pragmatism,
  author = {Vrahimis, Andreas},
  title = {Pragmatism and the History of the Analytic-Continental Divide},
  journal = {International Journal of Philosophical Studies},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {28},
  number = {4},
  pages = {541--554},
  doi = {10.1080/09672559.2020.1812974},
  abstract = {The history of what the title of Baghramian and Marchetti's book refers to as the `Great Divide' between Analytic and Continental philosophy has been the subject of much debate and controversy in recent years.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Vrahimis examines the role of pragmatism in debates about the analytic-continental divide, arguing that pragmatism complicates standard narratives because it shares features with both traditions. He traces debates about the historical origins and character of the divide, attending to the way that historiography of the divide itself shapes how philosophers in each tradition understand their own identity.

  RELEVANCE: This paper illustrates how the history of a disciplinary divide is itself contested, and how different historiographical narratives of the divide serve different philosophical interests -- a meta-level parallel to the appropriationism/contextualism debate about how to do the history of philosophy.

  POSITION: Historiography of the analytic-continental divide; emphasizes pragmatism's complicating role.
  },
  keywords = {analytic-continental, pragmatism, historiography, divide, Medium}
}

@article{shogimen2016elusiveness,
  author = {Shogimen, Takashi},
  title = {On the Elusiveness of Context},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {55},
  number = {2},
  pages = {233--252},
  doi = {10.1111/hith.10798},
  abstract = {How can we decide the pertinent context in which a given object of historical study should be examined? This question has long puzzled historians. In the field of intellectual history, the Cambridge contextual school represented by Quentin Skinner triggered a series of methodological debates about the relevant context for interpreting historical texts.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Shogimen argues that the concept of "context" in Cambridge School intellectual history is insufficiently determinate: there is no principled way to identify which context is the "relevant" one for a given historical text, because any text is embedded in multiple, often incompatible, contexts. The Cambridge School answer (linguistic context, illocutionary conventions) is one methodological choice, not the uniquely correct one.

  RELEVANCE: This is a sophisticated internal critique of Cambridge contextualism that clarifies its limits without endorsing appropriationism. It shows that the contextualist pole of the debate faces its own methodological under-determination problem, which is important for assessing whether a stable contextualist methodology is possible.

  POSITION: Internal critique of Cambridge contextualism; argues context is methodologically underdetermined.
  },
  keywords = {Cambridge-School, context, Skinner, intellectual-history, methodology, Medium}
}

@article{frazer2018ethics,
  author = {Frazer, Michael L.},
  title = {The Ethics of Interpretation in Political Theory and Intellectual History},
  journal = {The Review of Politics},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {81},
  number = {1},
  pages = {77--99},
  doi = {10.1017/s0034670518000967},
  abstract = {Scholars studying classic political texts face an important decision: Should these texts be read as artifacts of history or as sources for still-valid insights about politics today? Competing historical and ``presentist'' approaches to political thought do not have a methodological dispute---that is, a disagreement about the most effective scholarly means to an agreed-upon end. They instead have an ethical dispute about the ends of scholarship and the responsibilities scholars have towards their subjects.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Frazer reframes the debate between historical and presentist readings of classic texts as an ethical rather than methodological dispute: the question is not which approach better recovers meaning, but what kind of responsibility scholars have toward historical authors and toward contemporary audiences. He argues that neither approach is simply "correct" because they serve different and legitimate scholarly purposes.

  RELEVANCE: Frazer's reframing has direct implications for how to understand the appropriationism/contextualism debate: if the disagreement is ultimately ethical (about scholarly obligations), then purely methodological arguments for one side over the other beg the question. This is relevant to assessing the normative stakes of the debate in the anchor volume.

  POSITION: Ethical reframing of the historical/presentist divide; pluralist about legitimate approaches.
  },
  keywords = {ethics-of-interpretation, intellectual-history, contextualism, presentism, High}
}

@article{peden2019truth,
  author = {Peden, Knox},
  title = {Truth and Meaning in Historical Interpretation: A Davidsonian Approach},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {58},
  number = {3},
  pages = {327--341},
  doi = {10.1111/hith.12120},
  abstract = {This essay argues that Donald Davidson's work in philosophy sheds light on debates about truth, meaning, and context in historical interpretation. Drawing on distinctions between Davidson's project and that of his mentor, W. V. O. Quine, I aim to show that certain ambiguities that have arisen in the methodological reflections of Quentin Skinner and Frank Ankersmit can be resolved.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Peden applies Davidson's philosophy of language -- particularly his principle of charity and his externalist account of meaning -- to debates about historical interpretation, arguing that Davidson's framework dissolves certain ambiguities in Skinner's contextualism (about the relationship between meaning and intention) and Ankersmit's narrativism (about the relationship between historical description and truth).

  RELEVANCE: This article bridges analytic philosophy of language and the methodology of intellectual history, showing how debates within philosophy of language (Davidson vs. Quine) ramify into historiographical debates. This is relevant to understanding what analytic resources are available for mediating between appropriationism and contextualism.

  POSITION: Davidsonian philosophy of language applied to historiography; bridges analytic and Cambridge approaches.
  },
  keywords = {Davidson, hermeneutics, meaning, Skinner, Ankersmit, historiography, Medium}
}

@article{roth2016postnarrative,
  author = {Roth, Paul A.},
  title = {Back to the Future: Postnarrativist Historiography and Analytic Philosophy of History},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {55},
  number = {2},
  pages = {270--281},
  doi = {10.1111/hith.10800},
  abstract = {Jouni-Matti Kuukkanen has written an important book. It directly confronts a key theoretical dilemma that has shadowed debate in historiography for several decades: histories cannot be written without using some narrative structure or other, but epistemological evaluation cannot be applied to narratives qua narrative. Thus, if empirical inquiry takes the form of a history, then it cannot yield knowledge.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Roth critically examines postnarrativisism in the analytic philosophy of history, arguing that the attempt to evaluate historical accounts by non-narrative standards (e.g., by decomposing them into truth-evaluable sentences) fails to engage with what is distinctive about historical understanding. Narrative structure is not an accidental feature of histories but constitutive of how historical knowledge is organized and communicated.

  RELEVANCE: This article addresses the analytic philosophy of history and its relationship to narrative -- an issue that intersects with hermeneutics (Ricoeur's work on narrative) and with debates in intellectual history about how to organize historical accounts of philosophical development (diachronic vs. synchronic reading).

  POSITION: Analytic philosophy of history; critical of naive decompositionalism; supports attention to narrative structure.
  },
  keywords = {analytic-philosophy-of-history, narrative, postnarrativism, historiography, Medium}
}

@book{lapointe2023historiography,
  author = {Lapointe, Sandra and Reck, Erich H.},
  title = {Historiography and the Formation of Philosophical Canons},
  year = {2023},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  doi = {10.4324/9781003184294},
  abstract = {This book presents a series of case studies and reflections on the historiographical assumptions, methods, and approaches that shape the way in which philosophers construct their own past. The chapters in the volume advance discussion of the methods of historians of philosophy, while at the same time illustrating the various ways in which philosophical canons come into existence, debunking the myth of analytical philosophy's ahistoricism, and providing a deeper understanding of the roles historians of philosophy play in constructing philosophical traditions.},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lapointe and Reck argue, through case studies, that philosophical canons are historiographical constructions rather than natural kinds, and that analytic philosophy's self-image as ahistorical is a myth -- analytic philosophers have always engaged in canon-formation. The volume examines how different historiographical approaches (presentist, contextualist, hybrid) shape what counts as "the tradition" in different philosophical communities.

  RELEVANCE: This volume is directly relevant to the appropriationism/contextualism debate because it shows that both approaches have consequences for canon formation, and that choices about methodology in the history of philosophy are never politically or institutionally neutral. It also debunks the narrative that contextualism is a more "historical" approach -- analytic appropriationism also makes history, just differently.

  POSITION: Historiography of canon-formation; analytic tradition's historical turn.
  },
  keywords = {canon-formation, analytic-philosophy, historiography, High}
}

@article{beaney2018historiography2,
  author = {Mudrovcic, Maria Ines},
  title = {Time, History, and Philosophy of History},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {8},
  number = {2},
  pages = {217--242},
  doi = {10.1163/18722636-12341272},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mudrovcic examines the relationship between conceptions of time and the methodology of history, arguing that different theories of historical time -- linear, cyclical, kairotic -- generate different conceptions of historical intelligibility and different standards for what counts as a successful historical explanation. The synchronic/diachronic distinction in historiography reflects deeper commitments about how time structures historical change.

  RELEVANCE: This article provides theoretical grounding for the synchronic/diachronic distinction that arises in historiography of philosophy: whether to read a philosophical text as a moment in a temporal development (diachronic) or as a self-contained system with its own internal logic (synchronic) reflects assumptions about historical time. This is directly relevant to assessing when appropriationist "rational reconstructions" are appropriate.

  POSITION: Philosophy of history; temporal ontology of historical explanation.
  },
  keywords = {synchronic, diachronic, philosophy-of-history, time, Low},
  abstract = {In this paper, I intend to show that different ways of describing, representing or thinking about human affairs presuppose different types of consciousness of temporality. This proposal is embedded in the fruitful concept ‘régimes d’historicités’, which was coined by F. Hartog. Within this context and in regard to historiography and the philosophy of history, I will try to show that these disciplines and concepts, coined by these fields of study, are only possible in a temporal order governed by the future. Within this context, I will examine historiography, understood as the discipline which makes sense of human past and other disciplines, including the analytical or narrativist philosophies of history, which have yielded concepts such as the ‘historical past’, ‘historical consciousnesses’ and ‘historical time’.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{diggins2006lovejoy,
  author = {Diggins, John P.},
  title = {Arthur O. Lovejoy and the Challenge of Intellectual History},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Ideas},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {67},
  number = {1},
  pages = {181--208},
  doi = {10.1353/jhi.2006.0003},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Diggins examines Lovejoy's legacy as founder of intellectual history as a discipline, arguing that Lovejoy posed a challenge that remains unresolved: how to trace ideas across historical contexts without either collapsing into a history of individual thinkers (biography) or losing contact with specific historical agents and texts. The article traces how Lovejoy's successors in American intellectual history responded to and modified his methodology.

  RELEVANCE: This article provides intellectual-historical context for understanding why Lovejoy's "history of ideas" approach was institutionally and methodologically important, what it was reacting against (narrative philosophical history focused on great thinkers), and why it has been both influential and contested. The tension between tracing ideas and recovering agents' intentions is the same tension that structures the appropriationism/contextualism debate.

  POSITION: Lovejovian tradition; intellectual history; methodological assessment.
  },
  keywords = {Lovejoy, intellectual-history, methodology, history-of-ideas, Low},
  abstract = {Arthur O. Lovejoy and the Challenge of Intellectual History John Patrick Diggins Men and ideas advance by parricide, by which the children kill, if not their fathers, at least the beliefs of their fathers, and arrive at new beliefs. Sir Isaiah Berlin1 I was supposed to wind up the study of mine, and become the Lovejoy of my generation—that's the silly talk of scholarly people. Saul Bellow2 To become "the Lovejoy," with the implication that each generation could only have one, was the ambition of Moses Herzog in Saul Bellow's novel, written a few years after he had won the Nobel Award for literature. Herzog presents the life of a professor who has as many sexual escapades and as much personal angst as he has scholarly interests, too unfocused to be another Lovejoy. Bellow's novel is full of letters to Nietzsche, Heidegger, and other modernist minds who brought Western intellectual life to a standstill, posing a lasting question mark about the possibilities of knowledge. Bellow taught in the program of the Committee on Social Thought at [End Page 181] the University of Chicago. His colleague and close friend Allan Bloom also taught in that program. But when Bloom wrote the best seller, The Closing of the American Mind, he cited Nietzsche and Heidegger as part of the "German disease" that did so much to subvert academic thought, supposedly turning students into zombies, walking air heads responding only to vacuous visual stimuli and mind-numbing music, with Rock n' Roll blasting through a Walkman and having the effect of a "masturbational fantasy."3 While Bellow's epistolary novel interrogated modernist thinkers, Bloom's popular tract of the times cited many classical thinkers as a countervoice to the modernists—Aristotle, Plato, Socrates, Augustine, Aquinas, Dante, Hobbes, Kant, Lessing, Schiller, Locke, and Rousseau. Such minds also figured prominently in the works of Arthur O. Lovejoy. But the recitation of such names leaves the impression that Lovejoy concerned himself only with the main currents of European intellectual history. On the contrary, two of his most important and often neglected books are The Thirteen Pragmatisms and Reflections on Human Nature.4 There one finds profound meditations on two vital episodes in the history of ideas in America: the Enlightenment and the philosophy of pragmatism. Perry Miller, arguably America's greatest intellectual historian, skipped over the Enlightenment as he proceeded to study the Puritans and then the transcendentalists, and he never seemed to have much interest in the pragmatists.5 But what Lovejoy had to write on the Enlightenment and pragmatism is richly ironic. The pragmatists claimed to have liberated modern thought from the easy assumptions of the "Age of Reason," and today's poststructuralists charge that eighteenth-century Enlightenment thinkers deceived themselves in looking to the advent of knowledge as an answer to the presence of power. Lovejoy's books offer a different take entirely, making us aware that it is the modernist thinkers who may be deceiving themselves with the dubious promises of pragmatism and the arrogant conceits of poststructuralism and deconstruction, whose proponents claim to be the first "masters [End Page 182] of suspicion." In many of Lovejoy's works, nothing is resolved and everything is left suspended in a state of honest suspicion. Lovejoy was comfortable with contradiction, and hence more in tune with Augustine, who looked to the depths of human consciousness for spiritual knowledge, than with Aquinas, who believed evidence for God and human freedom could be found in reason alone. Nowhere is Lovejoy's affinity for the emotions of consciousness better seen than in his several essays on romanticism, both English, French, German, and even its origins in Chinese aesthetics and Confucian ethics. It was not reason and the notion of order and regulation that appealed to Lovejoy but the spontaneous promptings of nature springing forth without reflection or design. He delighted in quoting Voltaire against classicists and scholastics. "Worship God and practice justice—this is the sole religion of the Chinese literati. . . . O Thomas Aquinas, Scotus, Bonaventure, Francis, Dominic, Luther, Calvin, canons of Westminister, have you anything better? For four thousand years this religion, so simple and so noble, has endured in...},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{glock2008analyticHistory,
  author = {Glock, Hans-Johann},
  title = {Analytic Philosophy and History: A Mismatch?},
  journal = {Mind},
  year = {2008},
  volume = {117},
  number = {468},
  pages = {867--897},
  doi = {10.1093/mind/fzn055},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Glock surveys the widespread assumption that analytic philosophy and history of philosophy are incompatible---that analytic philosophy's commitment to systematic argument and progress makes it hostile to historical engagement. He argues this assumption is mistaken on multiple grounds: historically, many founding analytic philosophers were serious historians; systematically, knowledge of philosophical debates requires historical understanding; and practically, history of philosophy provides indispensable context for understanding philosophical problems. He distinguishes several reasons for doing history of philosophy and assesses each.

  RELEVANCE: This paper provides the most comprehensive analytical survey of reasons for and against the view that philosophy and its history are in tension. It maps the logical space of positions and assesses the arguments, making it essential for understanding why the question of the aims of history of philosophy arises specifically within analytic philosophy.

  POSITION: Defends the compatibility of analytic philosophy and historical inquiry, arguing that history of philosophy is philosophically necessary rather than merely decorative.
  },
  keywords = {analytic-philosophy, history-of-philosophy, mismatch-thesis, reasons-for-history, High},
  abstract = {In recent years, even some of its own practitioners have accused analytic philosophy of lacking historical awareness. My aim is to show that analytic philosophy and history are not such a mismatch after all. Against the objection that analytic philosophers have unduly ignored the past I argue that for the most part they only resist strong versions of historicism, and for good reasons. The history of philosophy is not the whole of philosophy, as extreme historicists maintain, nor is it indispensable to substantive philosophizing, as mainline historicists have it, it is merely advantageous (pragmatic historicism). Against the objection that analytic histories of philosophy are inevitably anachronistic I argue that it is possible to approach past texts with a view to substantive issues and in a critical spirit (contrary to historicist relativism and to misguided interpretations of the principle of charity). Indeed, such an analytic approach makes not just for better philosophy but also for better history.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{nichols2006worthStudying,
  author = {NICHOLS, RYAN},
  title = {WHY IS THE HISTORY OF PHILOSOPHY WORTH OUR STUDY?},
  journal = {Metaphilosophy},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {37},
  number = {1},
  pages = {34--52},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1467-9973.2006.00411.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nichols argues that most justifications for studying history of philosophy offered by historians of philosophy are inadequate. He distinguishes doing philosophy (arguing for and against philosophical theses) from doing history of philosophy (discovering what historical figures believed), and argues that the latter has value that does not simply reduce to the former. He surveys arguments---expanding the range of positions available, acquiring dialectical resources, understanding philosophical problems through their history---and finds most wanting as stand-alone justifications.

  RELEVANCE: This paper raises directly and in a focused way the question of whether history of philosophy has intrinsic or only instrumental value, which is a key question for this domain. Its critical survey of justifications is a useful map of the argumentative terrain.

  POSITION: Skeptical about standard instrumentalist justifications while leaving open whether intrinsic value arguments can succeed.
  },
  keywords = {value-of-history-of-philosophy, instrumental-vs-intrinsic, justifications, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract: Assume for the sake of argument that doing philosophy is intrinsically valuable, where “doing philosophy” refers to the practice of forging arguments for and against the truth of theses in the domains of metaphysics, epistemology, ethics, and so on. The practice of the history of philosophy is devoted instead to discovering arguments for and against the truth of “authorial” propositions, that is, propositions that state the belief of some historical figure about a philosophical proposition. I explore arguments for thinking that doing history of philosophy is valuable—specifically, valuable in such a way that its value does not reduce to the value of doing philosophy. Most such arguments proffered by historians of philosophy fail, as I show. I then offer a proposal about what makes doing history of philosophy uniquely valuable, but it is one that many historians will not find agreeable.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{watson2002whatIsHistory,
  author = {Watson, Richard A.},
  title = {What is the History of Philosophy and Why is it Important?},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2002},
  volume = {40},
  number = {4},
  pages = {525--528},
  doi = {10.1353/hph.2002.0085},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Watson argues that history of philosophy is uniquely and essentially historical: it is the history of philosophical positions, principles, and arguments without restriction of period or place. Its importance derives from the fact that philosophical arguments are timeless in a way that makes them always potentially relevant, but their historical embeddedness means that understanding them requires understanding their context of origin and development.

  RELEVANCE: A brief but direct statement of the view that history of philosophy is an autonomous discipline with its own methods and aims, not reducible to either philosophy or general history. Relevant to the question of whether history of philosophy is part of philosophy or a separate enterprise.

  POSITION: Autonomous discipline view---history of philosophy has its own character as history, while its subject matter (philosophical argument) gives it permanent relevance.
  },
  keywords = {nature-of-history-of-philosophy, autonomous-discipline, historical-approach, Medium},
  abstract = {Journal of the History of Philosophy - Volume 40, Number 4, October 2002},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{cohen1986doingPhilosophy,
  author = {Cohen, Lesley},
  title = {Doing philosophy is doing its history},
  journal = {Synthese},
  year = {1986},
  volume = {67},
  number = {1},
  pages = {51--55},
  doi = {10.1007/bf00485509},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Cohen argues for a constitutive relationship between doing philosophy and doing its history: the practice of philosophy necessarily involves engagement with the philosophical tradition, because philosophical problems are defined and shaped by the history of attempts to address them. To engage with a philosophical problem without knowing its history is to risk reinventing the wheel or, worse, to mistake pseudo-problems for genuine ones.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the question whether history of philosophy is constitutive of philosophy (rather than an optional supplement), staking out a position at the strong end of the "history matters" spectrum. This is a key position in the debate over whether the history of philosophy is dispensable.

  POSITION: Constitutive view---philosophy cannot be properly done without doing its history.
  },
  keywords = {constitutive-history, doing-philosophy, history-essential, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{williams2000humanistic,
  author = {Williams, Bernard},
  title = {Philosophy as a Humanistic Discipline},
  journal = {Philosophy},
  year = {2000},
  volume = {75},
  number = {4},
  pages = {477--496},
  doi = {10.1017/s0031819100000632},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Williams argues that philosophy should be understood as a humanistic rather than a scientific discipline, which has implications for how it relates to its history. Scientific disciplines can forget their history because they progress cumulatively; humanistic disciplines cannot, because their questions are structured by traditions of inquiry and the meanings of key concepts are historically constituted. Philosophy's situation is complicated because it aspires to systematic truth while depending on historically variable concepts. Williams uses the genealogical method---tracing the historical formation of concepts and practices---to illuminate philosophical questions without reducing them to mere history.

  RELEVANCE: Williams's distinction between scientific and humanistic disciplines provides a principled basis for why history of philosophy is constitutive of philosophy rather than merely informative. His genealogical approach also exemplifies the use of history for critique of the present, one of the key functional roles identified in this domain.

  POSITION: Moderate constitutivism combined with genealogical critique---history is necessary for philosophy because philosophy is a humanistic discipline, and history can serve as a critical tool.
  },
  keywords = {philosophy-as-humanistic, genealogy, constitutive-history, Williams, High},
  abstract = {Philosophy should not try to assimilate itself to the aims of the sciences. Scientism stems from the false assumption that a representation of the world minimally based on local perspectives is what best serves self-understanding. Philosophy must concern itself with the history of our conceptions, and we must overcome the need to think that this history should ideally be vindicatory. There is no basic conflict between arguing within the framework of our ideas, reflectively making better sense of them, and understanding their highly contingent history. The third of these activities is an inescapable part of the second.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{frede2021philosophicalHistory,
  author = {Frede, Michael},
  title = {The Philosophical History of Philosophy},
  booktitle = {The Historiography of Philosophy},
  year = {2021},
  pages = {36--43},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/oso/9780198840725.003.0004},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Frede argues that the history of philosophy is itself a philosophical enterprise, not a separate scholarly discipline. Past philosophers were grappling with the same problems that contemporary philosophy faces---problems about knowledge, being, value, and action---and their arguments remain philosophically relevant. A "philosophical history of philosophy" reads past texts as contributions to ongoing philosophical debates while being historically sensitive to the difference in conceptual vocabulary and intellectual context.

  RELEVANCE: Frede's position represents a philosophically sophisticated appropriationism that takes seriously both the philosophical content of past texts and their historical situatedness. His view that history of philosophy is philosophy itself (rather than a separate discipline) directly addresses the core question of whether the history of philosophy is part of philosophy or external to it.

  POSITION: Strong appropriationism---history of philosophy is itself philosophy, and the best history of philosophy is done by philosophers engaging philosophically with past arguments.
  },
  keywords = {philosophical-history, appropriationism, Frede, history-as-philosophy, High},
  abstract = {This chapter investigates the philosophical history of philosophy. It begins with a short account of its history before considering it systematically. The philosophical history of philosophy is best understood as a viable enterprise if one looks at it as a certain kind of retrospective history. Thus understood, the philosophical historian adopts a certain contemporary philosophical position as philosophically the correct position to take. They then proceed to look back at the history of philosophy to see how philosophers, step by step, advanced in the direction of the favoured position. This kind of retrospective history has certain inherent dangers and considerable limitations. There is the inherent danger that one takes the picture of the history of philosophy, which one thus gets, to be a representative picture of the history of philosophy. The enterprise thus interpreted seems to suffer mainly from the limitation that it yields a highly selective, rather anachronistic, and somewhat distorted picture of the history of philosophy.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{frede2021discipline,
  author = {Frede, Michael},
  title = {The History of Philosophy as a Discipline},
  booktitle = {The Historiography of Philosophy},
  year = {2021},
  pages = {164--171},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/oso/9780198840725.003.0015},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Frede examines the institutional and intellectual conditions under which the history of philosophy became a distinct academic discipline, arguing that this professionalization has had both benefits and costs. The cost is the tendency to treat history of philosophy as scholarship for its own sake rather than as a contribution to philosophy; the benefit is greater precision and rigor in reading historical texts. He argues that good practitioners move between the scholarly and the philosophical modes.

  RELEVANCE: Addresses the question of whether history of philosophy is a separate scholarly enterprise or part of philosophy by tracing the institutional history of the discipline---a meta-historical approach to a meta-philosophical question. Relevant to debates about the relationship between scholarly and philosophical aims in historiography.

  POSITION: Discipline-as-hybrid view---history of philosophy is neither purely philosophy nor purely history, but a discipline with its own methods that serves both masters.
  },
  keywords = {history-of-philosophy-as-discipline, institutionalization, scholarly-vs-philosophical, Medium},
  abstract = {This chapter discusses the history of philosophy as a discipline. Part of the confusion not only as to what historians of philosophy try to do but also as to how they ought to go about doing it, seems to be due to a misleading ambiguity in the term ‘history of philosophy’. Historically, it has been used in two rather different ways, each of which corresponds to a very different tradition of treating the history of philosophy, both of which persist to the present day, but which tend to get conflated. From roughly the middle of the seventeenth century onwards, we find treatises with the title ‘History of Philosophy’. These treatises show themselves to stand in a much older tradition that goes back to antiquity; namely, the doxographical tradition. But towards the end of the eighteenth century, a very different tradition emerges. As opposed to their doxographical predecessors, histories which adopt a chronological disposition are written out of the conviction that the philosophical positions of the past are no longer worth considering philosophically, that they are out of date. If they are still worth considering at all, it is because they constitute the steps through which we historically arrived at our present philosophical position.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{beaney2020twoDogmas,
  author = {Beaney, Michael},
  title = {Two dogmas of analytic historiography},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {28},
  number = {3},
  pages = {594--614},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2019.1693959},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Beaney identifies two dogmas that have governed analytic historiography of philosophy: first, that rational reconstruction is the appropriate method for analytic historians of philosophy; second, that contextual reconstruction is the appropriate method for proper historians. He argues that both dogmas are wrong and that good historiography requires integration of both modes. Drawing on Quine's critique of analytic/synthetic dogmas, he develops an alternative conception of historical methodology that acknowledges the mutual dependence of philosophical and historical concerns.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the appropriationism/contextualism debate by challenging its foundational dichotomy. Relevant to the project's focus on whether the opposition between rational and historical reconstruction can be sustained.

  POSITION: Rejects both pure rational reconstruction and pure historical reconstruction in favor of an integrative methodology.
  },
  keywords = {analytic-historiography, rational-reconstruction, historical-reconstruction, two-dogmas, Medium},
  abstract = {Starting from an analogy with Quine’s two dogmas of empiricism, I offer a (neo-Kantian) critique of two dogmas of analytic historiography: the belief in a cleavage between the justification of a philosophical claim and an account of its genesis and the belief in rational reconstructionism. I take Russell’s rational reconstruction of Leibniz’s philosophy as my detailed example.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{haakonssen2006historyOrPhilosophy,
  author = {Haakonssen, Knud},
  title = {The History of Eighteenth-Century Philosophy: History or Philosophy?},
  booktitle = {The Cambridge History of Eighteenth-Century Philosophy},
  year = {2006},
  pages = {1--25},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/chol9780521867429.002},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Haakonssen argues that the question "history or philosophy?" posed to the history of eighteenth-century philosophy is ill-formed, because the two enterprises are not alternatives but complementary modes of inquiry. He traces the distinctive character of eighteenth-century philosophy---its integration of historical, literary, and systematic concerns---and argues that this character demands a historiographical approach that is both historically rigorous and philosophically sensitive.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a case study of how the aims debate plays out for a specific period, demonstrating that methodological choices must be responsive to the character of the philosophical work being studied. Relevant to the question whether there is a single correct method for history of philosophy or whether method should be responsive to subject matter.

  POSITION: Period-sensitive pluralism---the right method for history of philosophy depends on the character of the philosophy being studied.
  },
  keywords = {eighteenth-century-philosophy, history-or-philosophy, period-sensitive-method, Haakonssen, Medium},
  abstract = {The history of eighteenth-century philosophy is a subject with its own history. However, the idea of what constitutes eighteenth-century philosophy has been remarkably stable over the two centuries that have elapsed since the period in question, and this stability has obscured the simple fact of its historicity and made it peculiarly difficult to question the historical adequacy of that idea. What is more, even now, when detailed scholarship has undertaken such questioning in earnest, tradition is so strong that works of synthesis and overview – not to mention teaching – have to pay it considerable respect in order to find an identifiable audience.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{perler2018alienation,
  author = {Perler, Dominik},
  title = {The Alienation Effect in the Historiography of Philosophy},
  booktitle = {Philosophy and the Historical Perspective},
  year = {2018},
  pages = {140--154},
  publisher = {British Academy},
  doi = {10.5871/bacad/9780197266298.003.0009},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Drawing on Brecht's notion of the Verfremdungseffekt (alienation or estrangement effect) in theater, Perler argues that history of philosophy should aim to make philosophical positions strange---to present past positions in ways that break the reader's habitual assumptions and reveal the contingency of present philosophical commitments. This defamiliarization function is distinct from both rational reconstruction (which seeks to engage past positions on present terms) and mere historical reconstruction (which seeks accuracy about the past). It is a critical-philosophical function served by historical engagement.

  RELEVANCE: Articulates one of the key functional roles assigned to history of philosophy in this domain---defamiliarization as a form of philosophical critique. This connects directly to Williams's genealogical approach and to the question of what philosophy can gain from history that it cannot gain from systematic philosophy alone.

  POSITION: Defamiliarization view---history of philosophy serves a distinctive critical function by making present philosophical assumptions visible as contingent.
  },
  keywords = {defamiliarization, alienation-effect, critique-of-present, Perler, historiography, High},
  abstract = {It has often been said that we should enter into a dialogue with thinkers of the past because they discussed the same problems we still have today and presented sophisticated solutions to them. I argue that this ‘dialogue model’ ignores the specific context in which many problems were created and defined. A closer look at various contexts enables us to see that philosophical problems are not as natural as they might seem. When we contextualise them, we experience a healthy alienation effect: we realise that problems discussed in the past depend on assumptions that are far from being self-evident. When we then compare these assumptions to our own, we reflect on our own theoretical framework that is not self-evident either. This leads to a denaturalisation of philosophical problems—in the past as well as in the present. The author argues for this thesis by examining late medieval discussions on mental language.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{vanAckeren2018philosophy,
  editor = {van Ackeren, Marcel},
  title = {Philosophy and the Historical Perspective},
  year = {2018},
  publisher = {British Academy},
  doi = {10.5871/bacad/9780197266298.001.0001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This collection brings together analytic and continental philosophers to examine the relationship between philosophy and its history from multiple perspectives. Contributors debate whether philosophy needs history, what the appropriate functions of historical engagement are, and whether defamiliarization, genealogy, or rational reconstruction best characterizes the proper relation of philosophy to its past. The volume is notable for including both proponents and critics of strong historicist positions.

  RELEVANCE: A recent collection that represents the current state of debate, with essays on defamiliarization (Perler), genealogy, and rational reconstruction. Useful as a survey of positions and as a source for the van Ackeren introduction and Perler essay directly relevant to this domain.

  POSITION: Pluralist---presents multiple competing positions on the aims and functions of history of philosophy without a unified editorial position.
  },
  keywords = {aims-of-history-of-philosophy, defamiliarization, genealogy, pluralism, Medium},
  abstract = {Since the rise of analytical philosophy, the relation of philosophy and its past is more hotly debated among philosophers than ever. Some scholars analyse historical texts without reference to current debates and their terminology, while others pursue first-order philosophy by focusing on problems instead of doxography—that is, without reference to their predecessors. A growing group, though, doubts that philosophy can be studied effectively on the basis of this sharp division. But does the study of the history of philosophy contribute to current philosophy? And, if it does, what precisely is the contribution? Does making such a contribution depend on using a specific method which determines how the historical perspective is related to systematic philosophy and philosophy in general? More generally, how do our assumptions about the relationship between historical and systematic perspectives affect our methodology and metaphilosophy or philosophy of philosophy? This volume presents and debates answers to these questions, which deserve to be addressed in their own right and not just as an adjunct to other discussions. The contributors of this volume provide diverse answers based on historical references, stretching from ancient philosophy to the most current debates, and also refer to various philosophical sub-disciplines.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{vanAckeren2018introduction,
  author = {van Ackeren, Marcel},
  title = {Philosophy and the Historical Perspective: A New Debate on an Old Topic},
  booktitle = {Philosophy and the Historical Perspective},
  year = {2018},
  pages = {1--17},
  publisher = {British Academy},
  doi = {10.5871/bacad/9780197266298.003.0001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The introduction surveys the history of the debate about philosophy's relationship to its own history, from Hegel through the analytic-historical turn debates to present discussions. Van Ackeren identifies three main positions: history of philosophy as dispensable to current philosophy, history of philosophy as useful but not necessary to current philosophy, and history of philosophy as constitutive of philosophy. He argues that the question of function is prior to the question of method.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a useful overview of the meta-philosophical debate and situates the various positions relative to each other. The claim that function is prior to method is directly relevant to the project's argument that positions on aims generate positions on method.

  POSITION: Meta-survey view---maps the positions in the debate and argues for the priority of functional questions.
  },
  keywords = {aims-of-history-of-philosophy, constitutive-history, dispensable-history, function-prior-to-method, Medium},
  abstract = {Since the rise of analytical philosophy, the relation of current philosophy and its past is more hotly debated among philosophers than ever. In this Introduction, I first explain the main questions of this debate: Does the study of the history of philosophy contribute to philosophy? What is this contribution? Is there a specific method relating the historical perspective to current philosophy? What does this mean for our view on philosophy in general? Second, I critically discuss doubts about the usefulness of the debate and defend its importance. Third, I briefly discuss the relation of the historical perspective and its relation to the philosophy of philosophy, and finally I summarize the evolution of the debate and some of its main positions.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{piercey2009usesOfPast,
  author = {Piercey, Robert},
  title = {The Uses of the Past from Heidegger to Rorty},
  year = {2009},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511576256},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Piercey examines how four major twentieth-century philosophers---Heidegger, Gadamer, MacIntyre, and Rorty---use the philosophical past. He argues that they share a broadly hermeneutic orientation that treats the history of philosophy as a resource for understanding present philosophical problems, but that they differ fundamentally in what they take that resource to be: for Heidegger it provides Destruktion of metaphysical prejudices, for Gadamer a tradition to be worked through, for MacIntyre a standard of rational superiority, and for Rorty an occasion for edifying conversation. Each use of the past embodies a different conception of philosophy's aims.

  RELEVANCE: Provides rich comparative analysis of different modes of philosophical engagement with the past, each connected to a broader conception of philosophy's aims. Useful for showing how positions on function (what history of philosophy is for) generate distinctive methodological and substantive commitments.

  POSITION: Hermeneutic plurality---multiple legitimate uses of the philosophical past, each tied to a different conception of philosophical practice.
  },
  keywords = {uses-of-past, Heidegger, Rorty, Gadamer, MacIntyre, hermeneutics, Medium},
  abstract = {In this book Robert Piercey asks how it is possible to do philosophy by studying the thinkers of the past. He develops his answer through readings of Martin Heidegger, Richard Rorty, Paul Ricoeur, Alasdair MacIntyre and other historically-minded philosophers. Piercey shows that what is distinctive about these figures is a concern with philosophical pictures - extremely general conceptions of what the world is like - rather than specific theories. He offers a comprehensive and illuminating exploration of the way in which these thinkers use narrative to evaluate and criticise these pictures. The result is a powerful and original account of how philosophers use the past.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{fischer2011therapy,
  author = {FISCHER, EUGEN},
  title = {HOW TO PRACTISE PHILOSOPHY AS THERAPY: PHILOSOPHICAL THERAPY AND THERAPEUTIC PHILOSOPHY},
  journal = {Metaphilosophy},
  year = {2011},
  volume = {42},
  number = {1-2},
  pages = {49--82},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1467-9973.2010.01680.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fischer distinguishes philosophical therapy (using philosophical method to dissolve philosophical problems that arise from linguistic or conceptual confusion) from therapeutic philosophy (philosophy whose overarching aim is to achieve mental peace or wisdom). He argues that history of philosophy has a role in both: historical investigation can reveal the genealogy of a pseudo-problem, thereby contributing to its dissolution, and the tradition of therapeutic philosophy from Hellenistic schools through Wittgenstein provides models for philosophy as a practical discipline.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the therapeutic function of history of philosophy---one of the key functional categories identified in this domain. Shows how engagement with past philosophical practice can itself be philosophically therapeutic rather than merely instrumental.

  POSITION: Therapeutic functionalism---history of philosophy serves the dissolution of pseudo-problems and the promotion of philosophical wellbeing.
  },
  keywords = {philosophy-as-therapy, therapeutic-philosophy, pseudo-problems, Wittgenstein, Fischer, Low},
  abstract = {Abstract: The notion that philosophy can be practised as a kind of therapy has become a focus of debate. This article explores how philosophy can be practised literally as a kind of therapy, in two very different ways: as philosophical therapy that addresses “real‐life problems” (e.g., Sextus Empiricus) and as therapeutic philosophy that meets a need for therapy which arises in and from philosophical reflection (e.g., Wittgenstein). With the help of concepts adapted from cognitive and clinical psychology, and from cognitive linguistics, the article shows that both philosophical projects address important and literally therapeutic tasks and explains how they can do so with genuinely philosophical argument and analysis. This brings into view new applications for philosophy, a need for therapy in core areas of the subject, and the outline of a new approach to meet what will be shown to be a central need.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{ooi2024permissivism,
  author = {Ooi, Daryl},
  title = {Permissivism and the history of philosophy},
  journal = {Metaphilosophy},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {56},
  number = {1},
  pages = {69--82},
  doi = {10.1111/meta.12712},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ooi connects debates in epistemology about "permissivism" (the view that multiple incompatible beliefs can be equally rational relative to the same evidence) to debates about historical methodology in philosophy. He argues that permissivism about methodological aims is plausible---there is no single correct aim for history of philosophy, and the various functions (rational reconstruction, historical reconstruction, defamiliarization, etc.) are compatible and can be simultaneously pursued by different practitioners.

  RELEVANCE: A recent paper that directly addresses the question of whether there is a single correct aim for history of philosophy or whether multiple aims can be equally legitimate. Useful for situating the debate about the aims of history of philosophy within a broader epistemological framework.

  POSITION: Methodological permissivism---multiple aims for history of philosophy are equally legitimate.
  },
  keywords = {permissivism, methodological-pluralism, aims-of-history-of-philosophy, recent, Low},
  abstract = {Abstract Permissivism is the view that for some body of evidence E there may be more than one rational doxastic attitude that inquirers may take towards some proposition. This paper examines the aims and processes involved in doing the history of philosophy. It argues that the complexities involved in the process of doing the history of philosophy motivates hermeneutical permissivism. Section 2 of the paper discusses and motivates complexity. Section 3 focuses on a particular kind of complexity that historians face, namely, the problem of theory choice. Section 4 argues that complexity motivates permissivism. Section 5 argues for the value of thinking about the task of the historian of philosophy as contributing to the landscape of plausible interpretations. The paper demonstrates how this way of thinking about our discipline may be productive and useful for historians of philosophy with differing aims.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{geuss2017changingSubject,
  author = {Geuss, Raymond},
  title = {Changing the Subject},
  year = {2017},
  publisher = {Harvard University Press},
  doi = {10.4159/9780674981980},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Geuss traces the history of philosophy from Socrates to Adorno as a series of contextually embedded responses to specific human situations and problems, arguing that what philosophy is "for" has changed repeatedly and that there is no single ahistorical answer. He identifies philosophy's relationship to power, to religious practice, to science, and to political life as shifting across contexts. The genealogical approach---tracing how philosophical questions and methods emerged from specific historical circumstances---is itself a philosophical argument against the view that philosophical problems are eternal and context-independent.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the genealogical-critical function of history of philosophy---using historical inquiry to challenge the assumption that present philosophical practice is natural or necessary. Relevant to the question of whether history of philosophy can serve as a critique of the present philosophical situation.

  POSITION: Genealogical critique---history of philosophy reveals the contingency and power-relatedness of philosophical practices, serving as a tool for reflective self-understanding.
  },
  keywords = {genealogy, philosophy-history, critique-of-present, Geuss, contextual-embeddedness, Medium},
  abstract = {For Raymond Geuss, philosophers' attempts to bypass normal ways of thinking—to point out that the question being asked is itself misguided—represents philosophy at its best. By provoking people to think differently, philosophers make clear that we are not fated to live within the stifling systems of thought we inherit. We can change the subject.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{davidson1984inquiries,
  author = {Davidson, Donald},
  title = {Inquiries into Truth and Interpretation},
  year = {1984},
  publisher = {Clarendon Press},
  address = {Oxford},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Davidson's collected essays articulate the principle of charity as a constitutive condition for all interpretation: we must presuppose that speakers are largely rational and largely correct about the world in order to get any interpretation off the ground. The principle is not a methodological choice but a transcendental prerequisite. Later essays distinguish between the Principle of Coherence (maximise internal rational consistency) and the Principle of Correspondence (maximise truth relative to the interpreter's worldview).

  RELEVANCE: For historians of philosophy, Davidson's framework is the backdrop against which debates about appropriate interpretation of historical texts proceed. The question of whether "charitable" reading means reading for truth, for coherence, or for a third thing (what the author meant) is directly at stake in the appropriationism-contextualism debate.

  POSITION: Foundational formulation of the principle of charity in its philosophically influential form.
  },
  keywords = {charity, interpretation, Davidson, philosophy-of-language, High},
  abstract = {In March of 2017, avideoof a small child standing in the street calling out to a broken water heater sitting curbside went viral on the internet. The water heater looked like a robot, with big saucer eyes and a cylindrical body, but only accidentally so. The child calls out to the robot and even when it doesn't respond, she hugs it and says, "I love you robot! I love you robot!"[1]What did we all love so much about this video? I'd argue it was the apparent social interaction between the child and the non-robot, a beautiful illustration of the ways in which people are often willing to engage non-intentional creatures or objects as social beings.
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

It should be noted, of course, that we don't need something to look like a robot (or animal, or creature at all) in order for children in particular, but also many adults, to engage with it as if they were engaging with a social being. After noting how many of my students still name their automobiles and treat them as intentional systems with agendas of their own, I once pasted googly eyes on the back of a chair for an entire semester in my artificial intelligence class, named the chair, and watched the students impose a personality and preferences on the chair over the course of the semester, simply because it appeared to have features only social beings tend to have. They began to include it in their considerations in the classroom, imagining what it might or might not desire, for example. What about these exchanges involves sociality? Does anything? There is an obvious epistemological versus metaphysical distinction to make here, and while it's easy to assume there is no being who is engaged in social behavior on the side of the "robot" or the googly-eyed chair, and we can know this pretty easily, there are definitely potential cases (if not any actual cases yet) where our epistemological limits prevent us from accessing the facts about a given potential social being.

This book sets out the project of doing just some of this background work on sociality, in order that we might not have to face constructed creatures designed for social interaction and wonder if they can engage with us on a genuine social level, or if they only appear to (acknowledging there are ethical implications for such a distinction from the outset, although the ethics are not really explored here at all). The stated goals in the introductory chapter don't entirely mesh with what I found in its pages, but it is worth exploring those goals briefly to make sense of the overall theme and project.

The editors offer this volume as contribution to what they call the new field of robophilosophy, which, they say, "aims to come to terms with the very idea of artificial social agency" (2). (I remain somewhat unconvinced that this new title is needed for work that largely seems to be cognitive science and human-robot-interaction, but I greatly look forward to watching this field emerge and carve out its space). The editors claim that social robotics is a new field that offers new opportunities to understand sociality, and they argue that inviting robots into our social world presents new sociocultural opportunities (3). This seems obviously true in one sense, but in another, we have always had artifacts that we treated as if they were social beings, even when we know they are not (and it is important to note that this claim carries with it enormous cultural baggage, since how humans interact with objects and apparent-subjects varies tremendously across cultures). Social robots do seem, at least, to be a tipping point in pushing the boundaries of what we might consider genuinely social, however, and this is one of the themes this book is concerned with. Importantly, the editors claim that this particular text is concerned with doing deep conceptual analysis of the sort normally confined to analytic philosophy, because, as they say, "researchers in analytic philosophy, and especially in social ontology, have yet to find their way into philosophy of social robotics" (3). This is worth noting for two reasons: first, because I was initially skeptical of their claim that so much of this work is mostly done from a continental perspective, explicitly not engaged by and in analytic philosophy (3), and, second, because much of this text turns out not to be doing analytic conceptual analysis of the sort they set out to do. This doesn't keep the volume from being valuable, however, and there is much to appreciate here.

It might have been useful for this book to be split into two sections: the first half as background conceptual analysis for sociality itself, and the second half as much closer to those cultural and social analyses (from the continental perspective) that the editors claim have already been staked out elsewhere. Again, there is good work here, but the promises made in the introduction did not meet the expectations in the text, and I worry that means the book won't find its audience.

The early chapters, in particular those by Johanna Seibt, Mark H. Bickhard, and David Eck and Alex Levine, are all indeed doing conceptual analytic work about how we should understand sociality itself. One prominent strength of this book is that each of those chapters on sociality seems to begin from a process metaphysics, rather than the historical particle- or substance-style metaphysics that is more common in analytic philosophy. Some (all?) of this is explicitly Bickhard's influence, and anyone who does work in artificial intelligence ought to welcome and embrace this work as it offers so many solutions to traditional problems in AI. Bickhard's chapter, in particular, is a good primer on his work in this area, and how embracing process ontology offers potentially non-controversial understandings of emergence and sociality itself. Eck and Levin further draw on process metaphysics to argue that interaction is prior to individuality and sociality, drawing also on the literatures on enactivism. Again, this is valuable work, but it is engaged in literatures around the concepts of alterity, autopoiesis, and participatory sense-making, which may be less comfortable to the analytic philosophers that I believed to be the audience of the book. These early chapters, engaged as they are in the foundational conceptual analysis of sociality, barely touch on applying any of this work to social robotics, leaving instead promissory notes that this work must be undertaken elsewhere. I find this realistic, given the depth and length of the chapters, but also unfortunate, as these early chapters are most promising on delivering the kind of analysis the book undertakes, but, apart from Seibt's chapter, doesn't deliver in the actual context of robotics.

Seibt's chapter is unique insofar as it may be the only one truly engaged in the project that the introductory chapter sets forth. Seibt sets out to offer a "general conceptual framework for the ontological classification of human-robot interaction," and really does deliver on this promise. What she offers is language and tools to make sense of our water-heater robot or our googly-eyed chair: what she calls the "soft problem in the ontology of social robotics" (14). That is, "the problem of how to describe human-robot interactions, from a second-person and third-person point of view, given that our concepts for human social interactions as such are inapplicable" (14). She argues that rather than simply generating and populating new categories to use in social robotic interactions, we need a new framework for "simulated social interactions" (15). Importantly, she distinguishes between at least three different sorts of scenarios within social interactions: 1) make-believe (wherein X treats Y as if it were Z), 2) fictional interaction (wherein "X interacts with Y as if it were doing Z") and 3) socially situated interaction (wherein "X takes Y to count as Z") (16).

These are clearly delineated, and the payout of the analysis is that she argues we commit a category error when we believe we can ever interact with something "as if it were a person" (20). Instead, it seems, we must be committed to treating it "as a person," a distinction played out in detail over the entire chapter. What Seibt hopes to do, and does with fair success, is to put forth a new set of what she calls "conceptual tools for forms of non-reciprocal or asymmetric sociality" (21). In other words, she suggests a new framework for understanding social situations in which we cannot, for whatever reason, attribute full human intentionality to at least one member of the interaction. One enormous benefit of this work is that, whether she has generated conclusively such tools or not, she has argued well that they are needed, and has given a clear starting point for how we might reconceive of social interactions so that we can make sense of both social robotics and human-human social interactions through this conceptual framework. This chapter offers people thinking about social robotics, and sociality more broadly, much to work with.},
}

@article{grandy1973reference,
  author = {Grandy, Richard},
  title = {Reference, Meaning, and Belief},
  journal = {The Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {1973},
  volume = {70},
  number = {14},
  pages = {439},
  doi = {10.2307/2025108},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Grandy introduces the "principle of humanity" as a correction to Quinean/Davidsonian charity. Rather than maximising truth, the interpreter should seek to minimise psychological strangeness: attribute to speakers the beliefs that we ourselves would hold given their evidence and inferential situation. This shifts the normative standard from truth to comprehensibility.

  RELEVANCE: The Grandy-Davidson contrast structures much subsequent debate about how historians of philosophy should approach figures whose views seem alien or inconsistent. The humanity principle is more sympathetic to contextual differences in evidence base, making it potentially more serviceable for historical interpretation than strong charity.

  POSITION: Alternative formulation of the interpretive norm, emphasising psychological plausibility over truth.
  },
  keywords = {charity, humanity, Grandy, interpretation, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{stern2016nietzsche,
  author = {Stern, Tom},
  title = {{``Some Third Thing''}: {Nietzsche's} Words and the Principle of Charity},
  journal = {The Journal of Nietzsche Studies},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {47},
  number = {2},
  pages = {287--302},
  doi = {10.5325/jnietstud.47.2.0287},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Stern argues that the principle of charity is not a single, neutral principle but is multiply ambiguous along three dimensions: the unit of interpretation (word, sentence, text, oeuvre), the mode (making sense, making consistent, making true), and the strength (minimal vs. maximal). This means charity cannot serve as a bridge between getting the historical meaning right and getting the best philosophy. Nietzsche is a particularly resistant case because his style deliberately resists coherent attribution.

  RELEVANCE: Stern's analysis crystallises why charity, as applied to historical philosophy, conceals rather than resolves methodological choices. The paper is directly relevant to the question of what it means to "read charitably" and whether charity is a determinate constraint or a vague gesture.

  POSITION: Critique of charity as applied to the history of philosophy; raises the question of what a non-distortive alternative would look like.
  },
  keywords = {charity, history-of-philosophy, Nietzsche, interpretation, High},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT The aim of this paper is to begin a conversation about how we read and write about Nietzsche and, related to this, other figures in the history of philosophy. The principle of charity can appear to be a way to bridge two different interpretative goals: getting the meaning of the text right and offering the best philosophy. I argue that the principle of charity is multiply ambiguous along three different dimensions, which I call “unit,” “mode,” and “strength”: consequently, it is not a single, neutral or independent principle to which we can helpfully appeal and it cannot, in itself, perform this bridging function. Nietzsche, I suggest, is particularly ill suited to some forms of charitable reading. I compare charity to what I call “misreporting” a philosopher's words, in order to highlight some problematic structural similarities. Finally, I assess what charity might be doing instead, if it is not intended to bridge meaning and best philosophy.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mercer2016descartes,
  author = {Mercer, Christia},
  title = {Descartes' Debt to {Teresa of {\'A}vila}, or Why We Should Work on Women in the History of Philosophy},
  journal = {Philosophical Studies},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {174},
  number = {10},
  pages = {2539--2555},
  doi = {10.1007/s11098-016-0737-9},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mercer argues, via a case study on Teresa of Avila's influence on Descartes, that recovering women in the history of philosophy is not merely a matter of equity but is philosophically necessary: it changes our understanding of canonical figures and their arguments. The exclusion of women from the canon has produced substantively distorted interpretations of canonical texts.

  RELEVANCE: This paper bridges feminist historiography and the main methodological debates by showing that canon exclusion has epistemic, not only political, consequences. It supports arguments for methodological reform that go beyond recognition to include recovery of suppressed influence networks.

  POSITION: Feminist historiographic argument combined with a positive case study; critique of canon formation on epistemic grounds.
  },
  keywords = {feminist-history-of-philosophy, canon, women-philosophers, Descartes, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{vannorden2017taking,
  author = {Van Norden, Bryan William},
  title = {Taking Back Philosophy: {A} Multicultural Manifesto},
  year = {2017},
  publisher = {Columbia University Press},
  address = {New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Van Norden argues that the exclusive focus of anglophone philosophy departments on European philosophy is intellectually indefensible and constitutes a form of epistemic injustice. He calls for the systematic incorporation of non-Western philosophy (Indian, Chinese, African, Native American) into the discipline, arguing that the current canon reflects historical and political exclusion rather than genuine philosophical merit.

  RELEVANCE: This book represents the most prominent recent statement of the case for canon expansion toward non-Western traditions. It directly bears on the question of what should count as the "history of philosophy" that historians study, and on whether the domain of the history of philosophy is constitutively Eurocentric.

  POSITION: Multicultural manifesto; challenges the disciplinary boundaries that define what history of philosophy is history of.
  },
  keywords = {canon, non-Western-philosophy, diversity, methodology, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{ganeri2025blueprint,
  author = {Ganeri, Jonardon},
  title = {Blueprint for Cosmopolitan Philosophy: {A} Post-{Eurocentric} Proposal},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2025},
  pages = {1--16},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2025.2511060},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ganeri proposes a "cosmopolitan philosophy" framework that replaces the Eurocentric paradigm of the history of philosophy with one that treats philosophical traditions from multiple cultures as equally valid objects and resources of philosophical inquiry. He distinguishes this from comparative philosophy by insisting on genuine philosophical engagement across traditions rather than juxtaposition.

  RELEVANCE: As a recent (2025) contribution from a leading figure in cross-cultural philosophy, this paper represents the current state of the argument for post-Eurocentric historiography, published in one of the leading journals in the history of philosophy. It provides the most up-to-date articulation of a position that bears on the scope and purpose of the history of philosophy.

  POSITION: Post-Eurocentric, cosmopolitan expansion of the discipline's scope; engages with canon formation from a non-Western traditions perspective.
  },
  keywords = {cosmopolitan-philosophy, non-Western, canon, post-Eurocentric, High},
  abstract = {I defend a cosmopolitan conception of philosophy.I construct a typology of cosmopolitan philosophical skill around a division into seven categories: assimilative, autonomous, comparative, diasporist, imperious, bicultural, and interjacent.I also try to begin to think through what kinds of reform of the academy will best serve to create institutions that foster the skills I identify as most helpful for philosophical progress.My paper is a re,ection on what the profession of philosophy must be in a culturally polycentric world, and on what new models of institutionalisation will best serve it in the future.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{harari2025historical,
  author = {Harari, Orna},
  title = {Historical Versus Philosophical Context: {Understanding} Ancient Philosophy in Its Contexts},
  journal = {Revue de {Synth{\`e}se}},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {146},
  number = {1-2},
  pages = {176--201},
  doi = {10.1163/19552343-14234071},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Harari examines Eduard Zeller's and Michael Frede's historiographical approaches and challenges the assumption that placing philosophy in its historical context avoids anachronism. She argues for an exegetical approach that studies texts in their philosophical contexts (not merely historical-social contexts), distinguishing contextualisation that facilitates understanding from contextualisation that facilitates judgment. This approach can minimise anachronism while remaining historically sensitive.

  RELEVANCE: This is a recent (2025) contribution that directly addresses anachronism as a methodological problem by distinguishing different types of contextualisation. It enriches the anachronism debate by showing that the historical/philosophical distinction is itself contested, and that both Zeller's idealist history and Frede's doxography involve contextualising moves that can distort.

  POSITION: Nuanced moderate position in the anachronism debate; calls for philosophical-textual contextualisation rather than purely historical or purely appropriationist reading.
  },
  keywords = {anachronism, contextualism, ancient-philosophy, methodology, Medium},
  abstract = {Through an examination of Eduard Zeller's and Michael Frede's historiographical approaches, I question the assumption that by placing philosophy in its historical context we can avoid anachronism. I argue that an exegetical approach that studies texts and places the discussions found therein in their philosophical contexts can minimize anachronism and offer a historically sensitive understanding. I clarify this claim through three case studies: the relation between John Philoponus' philosophy and theology, Aristotle's account of relatives, and Alexander of Aphrodisias' notion of mixture. In so doing, I distinguish contextualization that facilitates judgment from contextualization that facilitates understanding and show how the latter furthers our understanding of philosophical views in different ways.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{jardine2000uses,
  author = {Jardine, Nick},
  title = {Uses and Abuses of Anachronism in the History of the Sciences},
  journal = {History of Science},
  year = {2000},
  volume = {38},
  number = {3},
  pages = {251--270},
  doi = {10.1177/007327530003800301},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Jardine distinguishes multiple uses of anachronism in the history of the sciences and argues that some forms are indispensable for retrospective explanation and for establishing the significance of historical episodes. Not all anachronism is distorting; some is productive and unavoidable. He develops a taxonomy of anachronistic practices to guide discriminating use.

  RELEVANCE: Though focused on the history of science, this article provides the most careful published analysis of the forms of anachronism that historians may legitimately or illegitimately employ. Its taxonomy is directly applicable to the history of philosophy and informs discussions of when appropriationist readings commit the anachronism fallacy.

  POSITION: Moderate defence of selective anachronism; analytical rather than purely partisan.
  },
  keywords = {anachronism, history-of-science, methodology, Medium},
  abstract = {L'A. demontre que les historiens ont tendance a decrire les actions et les travaux passes avec un certain anachronisme et suggere qu'il serait utile de donner des limites concernant les presuppositions explicatives et interpretatives de chaque discipline scientifique},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{kaukua2010subjectivity,
  author = {Kaukua, Jari and {L{\"a}hteenm{\"a}ki}, Vili},
  title = {Subjectivity as a Non-Textual Standard of Interpretation in the History of Philosophical Psychology},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {49},
  number = {1},
  pages = {21--37},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1468-2303.2010.00526.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The paper identifies and defends "non-textual standards" of interpretation: heuristic postulations of features of the world or experience that must be assumed immune to historical variation in order to understand a text. Subjectivity is one such standard, and its use in the history of philosophical psychology requires methodological self-awareness given that contemporary philosophy of mind has contested what subjectivity means.

  RELEVANCE: The concept of non-textual standards provides a middle ground between purely contextual reading (which risks relativism) and purely reconstructive reading (which risks anachronism). It is relevant to the charity-anachronism cluster and to the broader question of how interpretation can be constrained without imposing present frameworks.

  POSITION: Moderate, analytically careful position that identifies a third class of interpretive standards beyond text and context.
  },
  keywords = {interpretation, anachronism, subjectivity, methodology, history-of-philosophy, Medium},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT Contemporary caution against anachronism in intellectual history, and the currently momentous theoretical emphasis on subjectivity in the philosophy of mind, are two prevailing conditions that set puzzling constraints for studies in the history of philosophical psychology. The former urges against assuming ideas, motives, and concepts that are alien to the historical intellectual setting under study, and combined with the latter suggests caution in relying on our intuitions regarding subjectivity due to the historically contingent characterizations it has attained in contemporary philosophy of mind. In the face of these conditions, our paper raises a question of what we call non‐textual (as opposed to contextual) standards of interpretation of historical texts, and proceeds to explore subjectivity as such a standard. Non‐textual standards are defined as (heuristic) postulations of features of the world or our experience of it that we must suppose to be immune to historical variation in order to understand a historical text. Although the postulation of such standards is often so obvious that the fact of our doing so is not noticed at all, we argue that the problems in certain special cases, such as that of subjectivity, force us to pay attention to the methodological questions involved. Taking into account both recent methodological discussion and the problems inherent in two de facto denials of the relevance of subjectivity for historical theories, we argue that there are good grounds for the adoption of subjectivity as a nontextual standard for historical work in philosophical psychology.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{tyson2014exclusion,
  author = {Tyson, Sarah},
  title = {From the Exclusion of Women to the Transformation of Philosophy: {Reclamation} and Its Possibilities},
  journal = {Metaphilosophy},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {45},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--19},
  doi = {10.1111/meta.12064},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Tyson identifies four argumentative strategies used in feminist "reclamation" projects (recovering women in the history of philosophy) and argues that three of them unintentionally reinforce the exclusion they seek to overcome, because they operate within canonical frameworks. Only the fourth strategy---transforming philosophical practice by attending to the mechanisms of exclusion---is genuinely corrective.

  RELEVANCE: This is a methodological contribution to feminist historiography that is relevant to the question of what it means to revise the canon. It matters for the project because it shows that simply including more figures is insufficient: the interpretive norms themselves may need transformation.

  POSITION: Feminist critique of reclamation methodology; argues for structural transformation over additive inclusion.
  },
  keywords = {feminist-history-of-philosophy, canon, reclamation, women-philosophers, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract In the mid‐1980s, feminist philosophers began to turn their critical efforts toward reclaiming women in the history of philosophy who had been neglected by traditional histories and canons. There are now scores of resources treating historical women philosophers and reclaiming them for philosophical history. This article explores the four major argumentative strategies that have been used within those reclamation projects. It argues that three of the strategies unwittingly work against the reclamationist end of having women engaged as philosophers. The fourth type, the one that seeks to transform philosophical practice and reconstruct its history, is the only strategy that will result in that engagement because it is the only strategy that pays sufficient attention to the mechanisms by which women have been excluded from philosophy and its history.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{dotson2012how,
  author = {Dotson, Kristie},
  title = {How is This Paper Philosophy?},
  journal = {Comparative Philosophy: {An} International Journal of Constructive Engagement of Distinct Approaches toward World Philosophy},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {3},
  number = {1},
  doi = {10.31979/2151-6014(2012).030105},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Dotson diagnoses a "culture of justification" in professional philosophy that requires practitioners from marginalised groups to constantly justify their work as philosophical to those who control canonical norms. She argues for a "culture of praxis" as an alternative that does not treat the discipline's self-understanding as fixed. The paper is itself a demonstration of the practice it advocates.

  RELEVANCE: While focused on professional practice rather than historiography per se, Dotson's analysis illuminates why canon questions are not merely historical but ongoing institutional and epistemic issues. It connects canon formation to the lived conditions of philosophical work and the discipline's gatekeeping mechanisms.

  POSITION: Critical philosophy of practice; diagnoses institutional mechanisms underlying canon maintenance.
  },
  keywords = {canon, professional-philosophy, diversity, epistemic-justice, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper answers a call made by Anita Allen to genuinely assess whether the field of philosophy has the capacity to sustain the work of diverse peoples. By identifying a pervasive culture of justification within professional philosophy, I gesture to the ways professional philosophy is not an attractive working environment for many diverse practitioners. As a result of the downsides of the culture of justification that pervades professional philosophy, I advocate that the discipline of professional philosophy be cast according to a culture of praxis. Finally, I provide a comparative exercise using Graham Priests definition of philosophy and Audre Lordes observations of the limitations of philosophical theorizing to show how these two disparate accounts can be understood as philosophical engagement with a shift to a culture of praxis perspective.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{smith2024historiography,
  author = {Smith, Daniel James},
  title = {On the Historiography of Philosophy and the Formation of the Canon},
  journal = {Idealistic Studies},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {54},
  number = {3},
  pages = {305--327},
  doi = {10.5840/idstudies2024826169},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Smith examines how canon formation in philosophy is a historiographical practice that involves both implicit normative judgments about philosophical value and structural mechanisms of institutional reproduction. He argues that attending to the historiography of philosophy reveals that the canon is not a natural reflection of philosophical significance but an artefact of contestable methodological choices.

  RELEVANCE: This is a recent (2024) paper that directly addresses canon formation as a historiographical issue, connecting it to the methodological debates about how history of philosophy should be done. It provides a close reading of the intersection between historiography and canon that is directly relevant to the anchor volume's concerns.

  POSITION: Critical historiography of canon formation; meta-level analysis of how philosophical value is attributed through historiographical practice.
  },
  keywords = {canon, historiography, methodology, history-of-philosophy, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper examines the formation of the philosophical canon in the period immediately after Kant. After a general introduction to the “historiography of philosophy,” it brings together three strands of contemporary scholarship in this area: a historical criticism of the empiricism/rationalism distinction that is often still used to understand early modern philosophy (Vanzo), histories of the exclusion of women from the history of philosophy in the late eighteenth century (O’Neill), and histories of the exclusion of non-European philosophy (Park). Though these scholars have different agendas, their studies share many conclusions, including the key claim that the little-known Kantian historian of philosophy Wilhelm Tennemann is the central figure in the formation of the standard story. The paper closes by comparing the main outline of Tennemann’s surprisingly familiar narrative of the history of philosophy with the standard text from before the Kantian revolution in the historiography of philosophy.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{schumacher2012adam,
  author = {Schumacher, Reinhard},
  title = {Adam {Smith's} Theory of Absolute Advantage and the Use of Doxography in the History of Economics},
  journal = {Erasmus Journal for Philosophy and Economics},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {5},
  number = {2},
  pages = {54},
  doi = {10.23941/ejpe.v5i2.105},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Schumacher uses a case study from the history of economics to illustrate how "Whig history" and doxographic reconstruction produce interpretive distortions. By reconstructing Smith as a forerunner of modern neoclassical trade theory, historiographers have misread his original views. The paper argues that doxography---presenting past views as anticipating present positions---is a methodological vice that compromises historical understanding.

  RELEVANCE: Though in the history of economics rather than philosophy, this paper provides a precise, verified case study of how doxography/appropriationism can distort historical scholarship. It illustrates the stakes of the anachronism problem in a concrete disciplinary context, useful for grounding abstract methodological debates.

  POSITION: Critical case study of doxographic distortion; supports contextualist methodology by demonstrating its consequences.
  },
  keywords = {doxography, anachronism, methodology, history-of-economics, Low},
  abstract = {This article reconstructs Adam Smith's theory of international trade and compares it with the way it is presented in modern textbooks as the theory of absolute advantage. This textbook presentation falls short of Smith's original ideas. I argue that the reason for this is the doxographic reconstruction of Smith's theory to fit him into a Whig history of international trade theory. In this way the historiography of international trade theory has falsely established Smith as a forerunner of modern neoclassical trade theory. I conclude by discussing to what extent Smith's insights can still be relevant today and what can be learnt from the mistreatment Smith has suffered in the historiography of international trade theory.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@misc{pickave2020defence,
  author = {{Pickav{\'e}}, Martin},
  title = {In Defence of Anachronism},
  year = {2020},
  howpublished = {Laval Th\'eologique et Philosophique 76(2):169--177},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Pickave argues that the history of philosophy has much to gain from engagement with the questions and conceptual tools of contemporary philosophy, and that such engagement need not constitute anachronism properly understood. He challenges the view that the historian's use of contemporary conceptual tools necessarily distorts historical texts, distinguishing anachronism as a vice from productive retrospective illumination.

  RELEVANCE: This short piece directly defends a moderate form of appropriationism by arguing that anachronism is not an automatic consequence of using contemporary philosophy. It provides a counterweight to strong contextualist arguments and bears directly on the debate about what historians of philosophy may legitimately do.

  POSITION: Defence of moderate anachronism; challenges the automatic equation of retrospective conceptual use with historical distortion.
  },
  keywords = {anachronism, appropriationism, methodology, Low},
  abstract = {Dans cette contribution, je soutiens que l’histoire de la philosophie a beaucoup à gagner d’un engagement avec les questions et les outils conceptuels de la philosophie (analytique) contemporaine. En particulier, je réfute l’idée qu’entrer en dialogue avec la philosophie contemporaine mène inévitablement l’historien de la philosophie à l’anachronisme. Quels que soient les risques d’échec, ils semblent être contrebalancés par les gains de connaissance rendus possibles par un tel dialogue. Les partisans d’une approche « purement » historique de l’histoire de la philosophie se défendent en soutenant que l’histoire de la philosophie ne peut et ne doit être étudiée que selon ses propres critères et indépendamment de nos intérêts philosophiques actuels. J’essaie de montrer que ceci est une illusion.In this short contribution I argue that the history of philosophy has much to gain from an engagement with the questions and conceptual tools of contemporary (analytic) philosophy. In particular I argue against the view that the historian of philosophy’s engagement with contemporary philosophy necessarily leads to anachronism. Whatever the risks of failure, they seem to be outweighed by the potential for insight. Advocates of a “purely” historical approach to the history of philosophy defend their approach by pointing to the idea that the history of philosophy can and should be studied on its own terms and independently of our current philosophical interests. I try to show that this is an illusion},
  abstract_source = {core},
}

